      "content": "The 20th-century labor movement championed democratic rights like the right to vote and free speech, effectively making unions one of the \"pillar sets of democracy\" as they empowered workers against corporate power. By securing collective bargaining rights and enacting pro-labor laws such as the Wagner Act and the National Labor Relations Act, unions shifted the political center of gravity back toward the left, which helped build a broader political consensus. This shift facilitated the creation of a robust middle class and a comprehensive welfare state, including the New Deal and the Social Security Act. In essence, the labor movement's strategy of \"making the politics of the American union the politics of the entire construction and building the middle class through labor power.",
